WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.678
Follow a catalogue request through an arrival queue,
application handling and a response.

00:00:05.678 --> 00:00:11.484
When all workers are busy, another request waits
rather than receiving an immediate worker.

00:00:11.484 --> 00:00:15.312
If arrivals remain faster than completions, the
queue grows.

00:00:15.312 --> 00:00:20.672
A bigger queue may absorb a short burst, but it does
not create processing capacity.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:27.120
Bounded queues make overload explicit and let the
service reject or defer work instead of allowing

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:30.658
unlimited memory growth and increasingly late
answers.

00:00:30.658 --> 00:00:36.948
The client sends an HTTP method, target and fields,
with optional content; the service returns a

00:00:36.948 --> 00:00:39.306
status, fields and optional content.

00:00:39.306 --> 00:00:45.137
A framework translates that contract into
application calls and manages network activity.

00:00:45.137 --> 00:00:48.864
It might use threads, processes or asynchronous
handlers.

00:00:48.864 --> 00:00:55.336
Those choices affect how waits are managed, but they
do not change the meaning of GET or a response

00:00:55.336 --> 00:00:55.794
status.

00:00:55.794 --> 00:01:02.004
In a design review, draw both the public request
contract and the internal queues: one explains

00:01:02.004 --> 00:01:07.234
interoperability, while the other explains what
users experience during a burst.

00:01:07.237 --> 00:01:13.165
Apply the chapter’s rendering pipeline to a
different service: a museum collection page.

00:01:13.165 --> 00:01:19.767
A browser requests an exhibit; the application reads
its description from durable storage, formats

00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:23.135
the description, adds navigation and returns HTML.

00:01:23.135 --> 00:01:29.601
The database owns saved content, while the
application constructs a representation for a
reader.

00:01:29.637 --> 00:01:35.899
Labelling these responsibilities makes the
architecture diagram useful: each arrow should
identify a

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:39.781
request or data transfer, rather than simply
connecting boxes.

00:01:39.781 --> 00:01:42.912
Now compare two visitors reading the same exhibit.

00:01:42.912 --> 00:01:49.173
They can share the description and public image, but
one visitor’s private saved-list indicator must

00:01:49.173 --> 00:01:51.553
not become the other visitor’s answer.

00:01:51.553 --> 00:01:56.523
This observation identifies possible reuse
boundaries before choosing a cache.

00:01:56.523 --> 00:02:02.449
Trace a second request after an exhibit description
changes, and explain which component must

00:02:02.449 --> 00:02:03.723
observe that update.

00:02:03.723 --> 00:02:10.031
The point of the historical diagram is this method
of reasoning about repeated computation, durable

00:02:10.031 --> 00:02:16.275
state and personalized output; its component names
are not a prescription for a modern deployment.
